addSubject({id:'eco', topics:[{"t": "Basic concepts: factors, PPC and circular flow", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain scarcity, choice and opportunity cost</li><li>describe factors of production and their rewards</li><li>draw and interpret PPCs (concave and straight), growth and unemployment</li><li>describe the circular flow of income and market structures</li><li>explain economies/diseconomies of scale and returns</li></ul></div><h3>1. Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost</h3><p>Wants are unlimited but resources are limited → <b>scarcity</b> → we must make <b>choices</b>. <b>Opportunity cost</b> = the next best alternative given up.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A student chooses to study instead of working at a shop for $20: the opportunity cost is the $20 (the next best alternative).</div><h3>2. Factors of production</h3><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Reward</th></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>natural resources (fish, land, sea)</td><td>rent</td></tr><tr><td>Labour</td><td>human effort (teacher, fisherman)</td><td>wages</td></tr><tr><td>Capital</td><td>man-made goods used to produce (boats, machines) – also financial capital</td><td>interest</td></tr><tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>organising and taking risks</td><td>profit</td></tr></table><h3>3. Production possibility curve (PPC)</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M40 30 Q200 30 230 150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M40 10 Q230 10 270 150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#c0392b\">U: unemployment</text><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"40\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#888\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"32\">X: unattainable</text><circle cx=\"167\" cy=\"58\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><text x=\"173\" y=\"70\">A: efficient</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"20\">Fish</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"168\">Potatoes</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">growth →</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A concave PPC: increasing opportunity cost</div></div><ul><li>Points <b>on</b> the curve: full, efficient use of resources. <b>Inside</b>: unemployed resources. <b>Outside</b>: unattainable now.</li><li><b>Concave</b> (bowed out) because resources are not equally suited to both goods → increasing opportunity cost. A <b>straight</b> PPC means constant opportunity cost (e.g. red hats vs blue hats).</li><li>An outward shift = <b>economic growth</b> (more resources, better technology, education).</li></ul><h3>4. Circular flow of income</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e6f4e2\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"79\">Households</text><rect x=\"230\" y=\"55\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fde2e2\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"79\">Firms</text><path d=\"M90 60 Q160 10 230 60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"28\">factors of production →</text><path d=\"M230 90 Q160 140 90 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"135\">← goods and services</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"52\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">← income (wages, rent…)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">spending →</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Money flows one way, real goods and factors the other</div></div><p>Leakages: savings, taxes, imports. Injections: investment, government spending, exports.</p><h3>5. Market structures</h3><table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Sellers</th><th>Product</th><th>Price control</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Perfect competition</td><td>many</td><td>identical</td><td>price taker</td><td>market vegetable sellers</td></tr><tr><td>Monopolistic competition</td><td>many</td><td>differentiated (brands)</td><td>some</td><td>restaurants, hair salons</td></tr><tr><td>Oligopoly</td><td>a few</td><td>similar</td><td>high, may collude</td><td>petrol companies, airlines</td></tr><tr><td>Duopoly</td><td>two</td><td>similar</td><td>high</td><td>two telecom companies</td></tr><tr><td>Monopoly</td><td>one</td><td>no close substitute</td><td>price maker</td><td>PUB electricity in Kiribati</td></tr><tr><td>Monopsony</td><td>one buyer</td><td>–</td><td>buyer sets price</td><td>only employer in a town</td></tr></table><h3>6. Scale and returns</h3><table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Economies of scale</td><td>average cost falls as a firm grows (bulk buying, specialised machines)</td></tr><tr><td>Diseconomies of scale</td><td>average cost rises when a firm is too big (poor communication)</td></tr><tr><td>Increasing returns</td><td>each extra worker adds more output (specialisation)</td></tr><tr><td>Diminishing returns</td><td>after a point each extra worker adds less (fixed factor crowded)</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Capital as a factor of production is…", "o": ["a natural resource earning rent", "man-made and used to produce other goods", "paid wages", "fixed in amount"], "a": 1, "e": "E.g. machines, boats, tools."}, {"q": "Who buys in the factor market?", "o": ["Consumers", "Government", "Households", "Producers"], "a": 3, "e": "Firms buy labour, land and capital."}, {"q": "Which pairing is INCORRECT?", "o": ["Labour–Wages", "Land–Rent", "Capital–Profit", "Entrepreneur–Profit"], "a": 2, "e": "Capital earns interest."}, {"q": "A point inside the PPC shows…", "o": ["efficiency", "unemployment of resources", "specialisation", "an impossible point"], "a": 1, "e": "Not all resources are used."}, {"q": "In the circular flow, savings are…", "o": ["an injection", "a leakage", "a factor reward", "a tax"], "a": 1, "e": "Investment is the matching injection."}, {"q": "GNE is less than GDP when the country…", "o": ["runs a trade surplus", "increases government spending", "receives remittances", "runs a trade deficit"], "a": 0, "e": "GDP = GNE + (X − M)."}]}, {"t": "Markets: demand, supply, price controls and subsidies", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the laws of demand and supply and their determinants</li><li>find equilibrium; calculate excess demand/supply, revenue and spending</li><li>show shifts of demand and supply on graphs</li><li>explain price ceilings, floors, taxes and subsidies</li><li>explain derived demand and factor markets</li></ul></div><h3>1. Demand and supply</h3><p><b>Law of demand</b>: price ↑ → quantity demanded ↓. <b>Law of supply</b>: price ↑ → quantity supplied ↑. A change in the good's own price causes a <b>movement along</b> the curve; other factors <b>shift</b> it.</p><table><tr><th>Demand shifts with…</th><th>Supply shifts with…</th></tr><tr><td>income</td><td>cost of inputs (wages, fuel, cans)</td></tr><tr><td>price of substitutes/complements</td><td>technology</td></tr><tr><td>tastes, advertising, health scares</td><td>weather, disasters (cyclone)</td></tr><tr><td>population</td><td>taxes and subsidies</td></tr><tr><td>expectations</td><td>number of firms</td></tr></table><h3>2. Equilibrium</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"15\">Price</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"168\">Quantity</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">D</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#c0392b\">S</text><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"85\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"88\">Pe</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"165\">Qe</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">above Pe: surplus</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#7a4fc0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#7a4fc0\">below Pe: shortage</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Equilibrium where demand = supply</div></div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Bananas: equilibrium $6, 25 kg → revenue = $150. At $4: Qd 30, Qs 20 → shortage of 10 kg. At $10: Qd 20, Qs 35 → surplus 15 kg; consumers spend $10 × 20 = $200.</div><h3>3. Shifts</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"15\">Price</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"168\">Quantity</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">D</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#c0392b\">S</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">D₁</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">demand ↑ → P↑ Q↑</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">An increase in demand (shift right): price and quantity both rise</div></div><table><tr><th>Change</th><th>Price</th><th>Quantity</th></tr><tr><td>Demand ↑</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td></tr><tr><td>Demand ↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td></tr><tr><td>Supply ↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td></tr><tr><td>Supply ↓ (e.g. cyclone destroys fleet)</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr></table><h3>4. Government intervention</h3><table><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Price ceiling (maximum, below Pe)</td><td>shortage, queues, black market; helps consumers (e.g. controlled rice price)</td></tr><tr><td>Price floor (minimum, above Pe)</td><td>surplus; helps producers (e.g. copra price support, minimum wage)</td></tr><tr><td>Indirect tax</td><td>supply shifts left: price ↑, quantity ↓</td></tr><tr><td>Subsidy</td><td>supply shifts right: price ↓, quantity ↑</td></tr></table><div class=\"warn\">⚠ A subsidy INCREASES supply (more at each price). The 2024 paper wrongly said \"fewer units\".</div><h3>5. Factor markets and derived demand</h3><p>In factor markets households sell land, labour, capital. Demand for a factor is <b>derived</b> from demand for the product: fish demand by a tuna cannery comes from consumer demand for tinned tuna.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Price $30, quantity 225 kg. Spending =", "o": ["$255", "$6750", "$7.50", "$675"], "a": 1, "e": "P × Q."}, {"q": "A price floor above equilibrium causes…", "o": ["a shortage", "a surplus", "no change", "lower supply"], "a": 1, "e": "QS > QD."}, {"q": "A subsidy to producers shifts supply…", "o": ["left", "right", "not at all", "up"], "a": 1, "e": "Lower costs."}, {"q": "One seller, no close substitutes, strongest barriers", "o": ["perfect competition", "oligopoly", "monopoly", "monopolistic competition"], "a": 2, "e": "Monopoly."}, {"q": "Firms in perfect competition are…", "o": ["price makers", "price takers", "monopolies", "government-owned"], "a": 1, "e": "Identical products, many sellers."}, {"q": "A few large firms dominate the market", "o": ["monopoly", "oligopoly", "perfect competition", "monopolistic competition"], "a": 1, "e": "E.g. airlines, telecoms."}]}, {"t": "Money and the money supply", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe functions and characteristics of money</li><li>define near money, M1, M2, M3</li><li>explain the quantity theory MV = PQ</li><li>describe the role of the central (reserve) bank and commercial banks</li></ul></div><h3>1. Functions and characteristics</h3><table><tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Medium of exchange (main)</td><td>accepted in exchange for goods – avoids barter (double coincidence of wants)</td></tr><tr><td>Unit of account</td><td>measures value; prices</td></tr><tr><td>Store of value</td><td>savings keep value</td></tr><tr><td>Standard of deferred payment</td><td>for loans and debts</td></tr></table><p>Good money is: acceptable, durable, portable, divisible, scarce, uniform. Kiribati uses the <b>Australian dollar</b>.</p><h3>2. Money supply</h3><table><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Includes</th></tr><tr><td>M1</td><td>notes and coins + cheque (demand) deposits</td></tr><tr><td>M2</td><td>M1 + savings deposits</td></tr><tr><td>M3</td><td>M2 + term deposits, bonds, longer-term deposits</td></tr></table><p><b>Near money</b>: assets easily turned into cash (savings, term deposits).</p><h3>3. Quantity theory</h3><div class=\"key\">MV = PQ   (M money supply, V velocity, P price level, Q output)</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Q = 30 000, P = $15, M = $100 000: nominal GDP = PQ = $450 000; V = 450 000/100 000 = 4.5.</div><p>If V and Q are fixed, more money → higher prices: inflation.</p><h3>4. Banks</h3><p><b>Commercial banks</b> (ANZ Kiribati, BoK): take deposits, make loans, create credit. <b>Central bank</b>: issues currency, banker to government and banks, lender of last resort, controls money supply (interest rates, reserve ratio, open-market operations, moral suasion). Kiribati has no own central bank because it uses the AUD.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Comparing the value of different goods uses money as a…", "o": ["store of value", "standard of value", "medium of exchange", "deferred payment"], "a": 1, "e": "Unit of account."}, {"q": "A farmer saves cash for later. This is money as a…", "o": ["store of value", "standard of value", "medium of exchange", "deferred payment"], "a": 0, "e": "Saving."}, {"q": "Everyone accepts money because it has…", "o": ["intrinsic value", "portability", "stability", "legal tender status"], "a": 3, "e": "Backed by law."}, {"q": "Buying both small and large items is possible because money is…", "o": ["durable", "uniform", "divisible", "portable"], "a": 2, "e": "It can be split into small amounts."}, {"q": "In MV = PQ, V is…", "o": ["volume", "velocity of circulation", "value added", "variable cost"], "a": 1, "e": "Turnover of money."}, {"q": "M1 includes…", "o": ["cash + demand deposits (+ traveller's cheques)", "savings only", "bonds", "large time deposits"], "a": 0, "e": "The most liquid money."}]}, {"t": "Inflation", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define inflation and measure it with the CPI</li><li>explain demand-pull and cost-push causes</li><li>describe effects on different groups</li><li>describe monetary and fiscal measures to control it</li></ul></div><h3>1. Measuring inflation</h3><div class=\"key\">CPI = (cost of basket this year ÷ cost in base year) × 100<br>Inflation rate = (CPI₂ − CPI₁)/CPI₁ × 100</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>CPI rises from 120 to 126: inflation = 6/120 × 100 = 5%.</div><h3>2. Causes</h3><table><tr><th>Demand-pull</th><th>Cost-push</th></tr><tr><td>\"too much money chasing too few goods\"</td><td>rising production costs passed on</td></tr><tr><td>lower taxes, govt deficit, more credit, wage rises spent</td><td>dearer fuel, imported goods, wages, raw materials</td></tr></table><p>Kiribati imports most goods, so world prices and the AUD exchange rate cause much \"imported\" cost-push inflation.</p><h3>3. Effects</h3><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed-income earners, poor households</td><td>lose purchasing power</td></tr><tr><td>Savers</td><td>real value of savings falls</td></tr><tr><td>Borrowers</td><td>gain: repay with money worth less</td></tr><tr><td>Exporters</td><td>less competitive</td></tr></table><h3>4. Control measures</h3><table><tr><th>Monetary (central bank)</th><th>Fiscal (government)</th></tr><tr><td>raise interest rates</td><td>raise taxes</td></tr><tr><td>raise the reserve ratio</td><td>cut government spending</td></tr><tr><td>sell government securities</td><td>reduce the budget deficit</td></tr><tr><td>moral suasion</td><td>price controls (short-term)</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Stimulus payments raised spending. This is…", "o": ["cost-push", "demand-pull", "deflation", "imported deflation"], "a": 1, "e": "Demand > supply."}, {"q": "Higher shipping and fuel costs causing price rises", "o": ["demand-pull", "cost-push", "stagflation only", "none"], "a": 1, "e": "Supply-side costs."}, {"q": "To fight inflation the central bank usually…", "o": ["lowers interest rates", "raises interest rates", "prints money", "cuts taxes"], "a": 1, "e": "Reduces borrowing and spending."}, {"q": "A weaker currency makes imports…", "o": ["cheaper", "dearer", "unchanged", "free"], "a": 1, "e": "Imported inflation."}, {"q": "Inflation hurts most…", "o": ["people on fixed incomes and savers", "borrowers", "exporters only", "nobody"], "a": 0, "e": "Their real income falls."}]}, {"t": "International trade, terms of trade and exchange rates", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain absolute and comparative advantage and gains from trade</li><li>calculate and interpret terms of trade</li><li>explain exchange rates, appreciation and depreciation</li><li>describe the balance of payments and trade barriers</li><li>compare foreign aid and investment</li></ul></div><h3>1. Comparative advantage</h3><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Banana Is.: 1000 t beef or 2000 t cassava. Sweet Is.: 1500 t beef or 2100 t cassava. OC of 1 beef: Banana 2 cassava, Sweet 1.4 → Sweet has comparative advantage in beef; Banana in cassava (0.5 beef < 0.71).</div><p>A country should specialise in the good with the <b>lower opportunity cost</b>, then trade. Terms of exchange must lie between the two opportunity costs for both to gain.</p><h3>2. Terms of trade</h3><div class=\"key\">ToT = (export price index ÷ import price index) × 100</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Year 3: 106/102 × 100 = 104 → improvement: exports buy more imports.</div><h3>3. Exchange rates</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"15\">Value of $</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"168\">Quantity of $</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">D</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#c0392b\">S</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">D₁</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">More demand for a currency (exports, tourists, investment) → appreciation</div></div><table><tr><th>Appreciation (currency rises)</th><th>Depreciation (falls)</th></tr><tr><td>exports dearer, imports cheaper</td><td>exports cheaper, imports dearer</td></tr><tr><td>balance of trade worsens</td><td>balance of trade improves</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>IC$1 rises from WA$0.70 to WA$0.95. Machine costing WA$200: before 200/0.70 = IC$285.71; after 200/0.95 = IC$210.53 – cheaper.</div><h3>4. Balance of payments and barriers</h3><p><b>Current account</b>: goods (visible trade), services and income (invisibles: tourism, shipping, remittances, fishing licence fees for Kiribati). <b>Capital account</b>: borrowing, investment. Barriers: <b>tariffs</b> (tax on imports: raise prices, protect local producers, cut consumer surplus), quotas, embargoes, subsidies.</p><table><tr><th>Foreign aid</th><th>Foreign investment</th></tr><tr><td>grants/soft loans from governments (Australia, NZ, Japan, EU)</td><td>private firms investing for profit</td></tr><tr><td>schools, health, infrastructure</td><td>hotels, fisheries, telecoms</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Comparative advantage is based on…", "o": ["total cost", "technology", "labour quantity", "opportunity cost differences"], "a": 3, "e": "Lower opportunity cost."}, {"q": "Which organisation promotes free trade?", "o": ["NATO", "IMF", "WTO", "UNESCO"], "a": 2, "e": "World Trade Organization."}, {"q": "Dumping means…", "o": ["illegal imports", "selling abroad below the domestic price", "destroying goods", "selling abroad higher"], "a": 1, "e": "Unfair competition."}, {"q": "Export index 1140, import index 980 (base 1000). ToT =", "o": ["860", "1163", "2120", "914"], "a": 1, "e": "1140/980 × 1000."}, {"q": "A tariff is…", "o": ["a tax on imports", "a limit on quantity", "an export subsidy", "a currency"], "a": 0, "e": "Makes imports dearer."}, {"q": "When a country's currency depreciates, its exports…", "o": ["become dearer", "become cheaper abroad and rise", "stop", "are unchanged"], "a": 1, "e": "More competitive."}]}, {"t": "Government: roles, budget, taxes", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe the economic roles of government (allocative, distributive, stabilisation)</li><li>describe types of intervention</li><li>explain progressive, proportional and regressive taxes and calculate tax</li><li>distinguish fiscal and monetary policy, expansionary and contractionary</li></ul></div><h3>1. Roles of government</h3><table><tr><th>Role</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Allocative</td><td>provide public and merit goods, correct market failure</td><td>roads, schools, hospitals</td></tr><tr><td>Distributive</td><td>make income fairer</td><td>progressive tax, pensions, copra subsidy</td></tr><tr><td>Stabilisation</td><td>control inflation, unemployment, growth</td><td>budget policy</td></tr></table><h3>2. Taxes</h3><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Rate as income rises</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Progressive</td><td>rises</td><td>income tax bands</td></tr><tr><td>Proportional</td><td>same</td><td>flat tax</td></tr><tr><td>Regressive</td><td>falls (as % of income)</td><td>VAT on basic food</td></tr></table><p>Direct taxes (on income, company profit); indirect taxes (VAT, excise, import duty). A good tax is fair, certain, convenient, cheap to collect.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Salary $3500 at 30% → tax $1050.</div><h3>3. Fiscal and monetary policy</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Expansionary (fight unemployment)</th><th>Contractionary (fight inflation)</th></tr><tr><td>Fiscal</td><td>cut taxes, raise spending → budget deficit</td><td>raise taxes, cut spending → surplus</td></tr><tr><td>Monetary</td><td>lower interest rates, more money supply</td><td>raise interest rates, sell securities</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Providing public goods like education and health is the…", "o": ["allocative role", "distributive role", "stabilisation role", "regulatory role"], "a": 0, "e": "Allocating resources."}, {"q": "Raising interest rates to reduce inflation is…", "o": ["allocative", "distributive", "stabilisation", "regulatory"], "a": 2, "e": "It stabilises the economy."}, {"q": "Which is a direct tax?", "o": ["Income tax", "Import duty", "Sales tax", "VAT"], "a": 0, "e": "On income."}, {"q": "Indirect taxes are…", "o": ["only for the rich", "based on income", "collected at the point of sale", "paid on wages"], "a": 2, "e": "E.g. VAT."}, {"q": "Revenue $10,000 and spending $12,000 gives a…", "o": ["surplus of $2,000", "deficit of $2,000", "balanced budget", "$22,000 budget"], "a": 1, "e": "Expansionary."}, {"q": "A budget deficit is what kind of fiscal policy?", "o": ["contractionary", "expansionary", "neutral", "monetary"], "a": 1, "e": "The government injects more than it withdraws."}]}, {"t": "Inequality and unemployment", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>measure income inequality (Lorenz curve, Gini)</li><li>explain causes and effects of inequality</li><li>define types of unemployment and their causes</li><li>describe measures to reduce unemployment and inequality</li></ul></div><h3>1. Inequality</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 220 200\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"30\" y=\"20\" width=\"160\" height=\"160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M30 180 Q150 175 190 20\" fill=\"#d4a72c44\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">equality</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#c0392b\">Lorenz curve</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"198\">% of population</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"15\">% income</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The further the Lorenz curve from the diagonal, the greater the inequality (higher Gini)</div></div><p>Causes: unequal wealth and land, education, skills, jobs in towns vs islands. Reduce with progressive tax, welfare, free education, minimum wage.</p><h3>2. Unemployment</h3><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th></tr><tr><td>Frictional</td><td>between jobs</td></tr><tr><td>Structural</td><td>skills no longer needed (industry declines)</td></tr><tr><td>Cyclical</td><td>recession, low demand</td></tr><tr><td>Seasonal</td><td>work only in some seasons</td></tr><tr><td>Disguised / underemployment</td><td>working but not fully productive (subsistence)</td></tr></table><p>Voluntary = choose not to work at current wage; involuntary = want work but can't find it.</p><table><tr><th>Measure</th><th>How it helps</th></tr><tr><td>Government spending on projects</td><td>creates jobs directly</td></tr><tr><td>Subsidies to firms</td><td>lower costs → hire more</td></tr><tr><td>Training and education</td><td>fixes structural unemployment</td></tr><tr><td>Labour mobility schemes (RSE NZ, PALM Australia)</td><td>jobs abroad, remittances</td></tr><tr><td>Lower retirement age / raise school-leaving age</td><td>fewer in labour force</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Giving extra help to students who need it is…", "o": ["equality", "equity", "inequality", "efficiency"], "a": 1, "e": "Fairness according to need."}, {"q": "A progressive tax…", "o": ["takes a higher % from higher incomes", "is the same for all", "is a tax on imports", "is a subsidy"], "a": 0, "e": "It reduces inequality."}, {"q": "Redistribution moves the Lorenz curve…", "o": ["further from the line of equality", "closer to the line of equality", "off the graph", "nowhere"], "a": 1, "e": "More equal."}, {"q": "People willing to work at the current wage but with no job are…", "o": ["voluntarily unemployed", "involuntarily unemployed", "retired", "students"], "a": 1, "e": "Involuntary."}, {"q": "Lower interest rates reduce unemployment because…", "o": ["saving rises", "borrowing and spending rise, so firms hire", "taxes rise", "imports fall"], "a": 1, "e": "Loose monetary policy."}]}, {"t": "Economic development", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe features of developing and developed countries</li><li>use indicators (real GDP per capita, HDI, literacy, life expectancy)</li><li>explain factors that encourage or hinder growth</li><li>evaluate strategies for development</li></ul></div><h3>1. Measuring development</h3><div class=\"key\">GDP per capita = GDP ÷ population<br>Real GDP per capita (adjusted for inflation) is the best single income measure</div><table><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Developed</th><th>Developing</th></tr><tr><td>GDP per capita</td><td>high</td><td>low</td></tr><tr><td>Life expectancy</td><td>80+</td><td>60–70</td></tr><tr><td>Literacy</td><td>~99%</td><td>lower</td></tr><tr><td>Main sector</td><td>tertiary (services)</td><td>primary (farming, fishing)</td></tr><tr><td>Population growth</td><td>slow</td><td>fast</td></tr></table><p><b>HDI</b> (Human Development Index) combines income, health (life expectancy) and education.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Highland: GDP $500 m, population 1.5 m → $333 per person. Aid $20 m : GDP $500 m = 1 : 25 (4%).</div><h3>2. Growth factors</h3><table><tr><th>Encourage</th><th>Hinder</th></tr><tr><td>investment in capital goods</td><td>brain drain of skilled workers</td></tr><tr><td>education and health</td><td>low savings and investment</td></tr><tr><td>good governance, stability</td><td>corruption, conflict</td></tr><tr><td>infrastructure, technology</td><td>remoteness, small markets, disasters, climate change</td></tr><tr><td>foreign investment and aid</td><td>falling export prices</td></tr></table><p>Kiribati's challenges: isolation, small land, few resources apart from fish, climate change. Strengths: huge fishing zone (licence fees), the RERF sovereign wealth fund, remittances.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "The main goal of economic development", "o": ["population growth", "national defence", "bigger government", "raise living standards and reduce poverty"], "a": 3, "e": "Wellbeing."}, {"q": "NOT an indicator of development", "o": ["GDP per capita", "life expectancy", "literacy rate", "inflation rate"], "a": 3, "e": "Inflation measures prices."}, {"q": "Which provides development loans?", "o": ["IMF", "WTO", "NATO", "World Bank"], "a": 3, "e": "World Bank."}, {"q": "Best for sustainable development", "o": ["more mining", "overusing resources", "renewable energy", "short-term profit"], "a": 2, "e": "Solar, wind."}, {"q": "GDP $600m, population 50,000. GDP per capita?", "o": ["$12,000", "$1,200", "$120,000", "$30,000"], "a": 0, "e": "600,000,000/50,000."}, {"q": "HDI combines…", "o": ["life expectancy, education, income", "exports, imports, aid", "inflation, interest, tax", "population only"], "a": 0, "e": "Three dimensions."}]}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC Economics 2025.pdf', note:'Graph-based answers use values from the graphs in the paper (also used by the marking scheme). Open the original to see the graphs.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Basic concepts (53 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1. Capital characteristic 2. Buyer in the factor market 3. Incorrect factor–income pair 4. GNE < GDP when… 5. Foreign exchange market',a:'1. <b>B</b>: man-made and used to produce other goods\n2. <b>D</b>: producer (firms buy factors)\n3. <b>C</b>: capital earns interest, not profit\n4. <b>A</b>: the country runs a trade surplus (GDP = GNE + X − M)\n5. <b>A</b>: a global market for buying and selling currencies'},
{n:'6a)',q:'Two assumptions of the PPC.',a:'(i) Resources are fixed and fully employed.\n(ii) Technology is fixed. (Also: only two goods are produced.)'},
{n:'6b)',q:'Explain the shape of the PPC.',a:'It is <b>concave (bowed outward)</b> because resources are not equally suited to producing fish and crabs. As more fish are produced, resources better suited to crabs must be moved, so the opportunity cost increases.'},
{n:'6c)',q:'Significance of points A to D, point A, point E.',a:'(i) A–D: on the curve, all resources are fully and efficiently used (maximum output combinations).\n(ii) A: complete specialisation, with all resources producing only one good (fish).\n(iii) E: inside the curve, resources are unemployed or underused (inefficient).'},
{n:'6d)',q:'How does the PPC illustrate scarcity?',a:'The curve slopes downward and its boundary marks the limit of output. Because resources are limited, producing more fish means giving up some crabs. We cannot have everything, which is scarcity and opportunity cost.'},
{n:'6e)',q:'Opportunity cost of the first 30,000 fish.',a:'Crabs given up = 70,000 − 60,000 = <b>10,000 crabs</b> (read from the curve).'},
{n:'7a)',q:'Circular flow: identify A–D.',a:'A = <b>firms/producers</b>\nB = <b>factor incomes</b> (wages, rent, interest, profit)\nC = <b>savings</b>\nD = <b>investment</b>'},
{n:'7b)',q:'Major role of the financial sector.',a:'It collects savings from households (a leakage) and lends them to firms for investment (an injection), channelling unused income back into production.'},
{n:'7c)',q:'(i) Income tax vs VAT (ii) effect of higher taxes on households.',a:'(i) Income tax is a direct tax on people\'s earnings. VAT is an indirect tax added to the price of goods and services when they are sold.\n(ii) Disposable income falls → consumption falls → firms\' sales and production fall → the flow of income gets smaller (taxes are a leakage).'},
{n:'8a–c)',q:'(a) Strongest barriers, no close substitutes (b) oligopoly product (c) number of sellers.',a:'(a) <b>Monopoly</b>\n(b) Standardised (e.g. cement, fuel) or differentiated (e.g. phones, airlines)\n(c) Monopolistic competition: many sellers. Monopoly: one seller.'},
{n:'8d)',q:'Why does perfect competition have no barriers to entry?',a:'There are many small firms selling identical products, with perfect knowledge and equal access to resources. No firm is big enough to block others, and there are no legal or cost barriers, so firms can enter and leave freely. Each is a price taker.'},
{n:'9a)',q:'Consumer spending at $30.',a:'Spending = P × Q = $30 × 225 = <b>$6,750</b>'},
{n:'9b)',q:'Price floor $40: (ii) market situation (iii) change in quantity supplied and in firms\' revenue.',a:'(ii) A <b>surplus</b> (excess supply) of 125 kg: QS rises to 300 kg while QD falls.\n(iii) Change in QS = 300 − 225 = <b>75 kg</b> more\nChange in revenue = ($40 × 300) − ($30 × 225) = $12,000 − $6,750 = <b>+$5,250</b>', flag:'This revenue answer assumes the government buys the whole surplus, so firms sell all 300 kg. If only consumers buy, firms sell just QD at $40.'},
{n:'9c)',q:'$5 subsidy: (i) new supply curve (ii) benefits to consumers (iii) price received by firms and government cost.',a:'(i) Shift the supply curve DOWN/RIGHT by $5 at every quantity and label it Ss.\n(ii) Consumers pay a LOWER price and buy a LARGER quantity.\n(iii) Price received by firms = new equilibrium price + $5.\nGovernment spending = $5 × new equilibrium quantity. (Read the new equilibrium from your graph.)'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Money (33 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'Functions and characteristics of money; bond maturing in 2 years.',a:'1. <b>B</b>: standard of value\n2. <b>A</b>: store of value\n3. <b>D</b>: legal tender status\n4. <b>C</b>: divisibility\n5. <b>C</b>: M3 (per the marking scheme)'},
{n:'6a–b)',q:'V in MV = PQ; P when M = $500m, V = 4, Q = $1000m.',a:'V = <b>velocity of circulation</b> (the number of times money changes hands)\nP = MV/Q = (500 × 4)/1000 = <b>2</b>'},
{n:'6c)',q:'The $100 passes Terira → Tekooki → Amon → telephone company.',a:'(i) <b>3 times</b>\n(ii) Total transactions = $100 × 3 = <b>$300</b>'},
{n:'7a)',q:'M1, M2, M3.',a:'M1 = cash 500 + traveller\'s cheques 100 + demand deposits 120 = <b>$720m</b>\nM2 = M1 + savings 200 + small time deposits 700 = <b>$1,620m</b>\nM3 = M2 + other deposits 1,200 = <b>$2,820m</b>'},
{n:'7b)',q:'Effect on money supply: (i) government borrowing from the central bank (ii) more foreign aid.',a:'(i) <b>Increases</b>: the central bank creates new money for the government, which spends it. Recipients deposit it in banks, so the money supply grows.\n(ii) <b>Increases</b>: foreign currency flows in and is converted and spent locally, adding to money in the economy.'},
{n:'8a)',q:'COVID-19: (i) demand-pull (ii) interest rates (iii) cost-push.',a:'(i) Stimulus payments and low interest rates boosted household incomes and spending, while output was limited, so aggregate demand exceeded supply and prices rose.\n(ii) The central bank raises interest rates: borrowing becomes expensive and saving attractive, so spending and AD fall and inflation eases.\n(iii) Lockdowns closed factories, supply chains broke down and shipping costs soared, raising production and transport costs. Firms passed these on as higher prices.'},
{n:'8b)',q:'Three causes of inflation in Islandia.',a:'1. <b>Higher government spending</b> (infrastructure, public wages) → AD rises → demand-pull inflation.\n2. <b>Rising world fuel prices</b> → transport and production costs rise → cost-push inflation.\n3. <b>Currency depreciation</b> → imported food and fuel cost more → imported inflation.'}
]},
{t:'Section C – International trade (40 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'Absolute advantage, comparative advantage, WTO, specialisation, dumping.',a:'1. <b>B</b> 2. <b>D</b> 3. <b>C</b> 4. <b>A</b> 5. <b>B</b>'},
{n:'6a–b)',q:'Terms of trade formula; ToT for 2001 and 2002.',a:'ToT = (export price index ÷ import price index) × 1000 (the base used in the table)\n2001: 1140/980 × 1000 = <b>1163</b>\n2002: 1010/1105 × 1000 = <b>914</b>'},
{n:'6c–d)',q:'Year with an unfavourable movement; interpret above, below and equal to the base.',a:'c) <b>2002</b> (fell from 1163 to 914). 2003 also fell, to 899.\nd) (i) Above base: <b>favourable</b> (ii) Below base: <b>unfavourable</b> (iii) Equal: <b>neutral</b>'},
{n:'6e)',q:'One policy to improve deteriorating terms of trade.',a:'Diversify and add value to exports (e.g. process fish into canned or loined products, or coconut oil instead of raw copra) so export prices rise. Or reduce dependence on expensive imports by producing more food locally.'},
{n:'7a–b)',q:'PICTA; free trade vs protectionism.',a:'PICTA = <b>Pacific Island Countries Trade Agreement</b>.\nFree trade: goods and services move between countries with no barriers. Protectionism: governments use barriers (tariffs, quotas) to restrict imports and protect local industry.'},
{n:'7c–d)',q:'One argument for free trade; why infant industries may oppose it.',a:'c) Countries specialise in what they produce at the lowest opportunity cost, so world output rises and consumers get cheaper goods and more choice.\nd) New (infant) Pacific industries have high costs and small scale. They cannot compete with cheap imports from large overseas firms and may close before they grow, so they want temporary protection.'},
{n:'7e)',q:'(i) An import-restricting measure (ii) an export-increasing measure.',a:'(i) <b>Tariff</b>: a tax on imports makes them dearer, so fewer are bought. (Or a quota: a limit on the quantity imported.)\n(ii) <b>Export subsidy</b>: the government pays exporters part of their costs, so they can sell more cheaply on world markets. (Or low-interest export finance, or duty concessions on inputs.)'},
{n:'7f)',q:'How protectionism benefits import-competing industries and workers.',a:'Tariffs and quotas raise the price or limit the quantity of competing imports. Demand shifts to local products, so local firms sell more, earn more revenue and stay open. They keep or hire workers, protecting jobs and incomes.'},
{n:'8a)',q:'Appreciation vs depreciation.',a:'Appreciation: a currency rises in value against another (buys more foreign currency). Depreciation: it falls in value (buys less).'},
{n:'8b)',q:'Generator worth CI$1,000: cost in SI$ before ($1SI = $2CI) and after ($1SI = $3CI).',a:'(i) 1000 ÷ 2 = <b>SI$500</b>\n(ii) 1000 ÷ 3 = <b>SI$333.33</b>'},
{n:'8c)',q:'Effects of Candy Island\'s depreciation on (i) its exports (ii) its imports.',a:'(i) Exports become cheaper for foreign buyers, so demand and export volumes <b>increase</b>.\n(ii) Imports become more expensive in CI dollars, so imports <b>decrease</b>.'}
]},
{t:'Section D – Government (47 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'Allocative role, distributive role, stabilisation, direct tax, indirect tax.',a:'1. <b>D</b> 2. <b>B</b> 3. <b>D</b> 4. <b>A</b> 5. <b>C</b>'},
{n:'6a–b)',q:'Type of budget; calculate it (revenue $10,000, spending $12,000).',a:'(a) <b>Budget deficit</b>\n(b) Balance = revenue − expenditure = $10,000 − $12,000 = <b>−$2,000</b>'},
{n:'6c–d)',q:'Type of fiscal policy; effect on the economy.',a:'(c) <b>Expansionary fiscal policy</b>: the government spends more than it collects, adding to aggregate demand.\n(d) More spending → more income for households and firms → more consumption, production and employment (growth). Risks: inflation if demand outruns supply, and rising government debt.', flag:'The marking scheme calls this "contractionary fiscal policy". That is wrong: a deficit (spending > revenue) is EXPANSIONARY.'},
{n:'7a–b)',q:'Equality vs equity; equity in education.',a:'a) Equality = everyone receives the same. Equity = fairness: support is given according to need so everyone can reach the same outcome.\nb) Students who need it get extra support, e.g. remedial reading classes, audio books, ramps for disabled students, scholarships or boarding for outer-island students, so all can learn at the same level.'},
{n:'7c–e)',q:'Two policies to reduce income inequality; how they work; the Lorenz curve.',a:'(i) <b>Progressive income tax</b>: higher earners pay a higher percentage, which narrows the gap after tax.\n(ii) <b>Transfer payments</b> (e.g. senior citizens\' and disability benefits, copra subsidy): income is given to low-income groups, raising their share.\n(e) Draw the new Lorenz curve CLOSER to the 45° line of equality than the original.'},
{n:'8a–b)',q:'Minimum wage: voluntary unemployment letter; effects on employment, voluntary and involuntary unemployment.',a:'(a) <b>C</b> (per the marking scheme/graph)\n(b)(i) Actual employment <b>falls</b>: labour is dearer, so firms hire fewer workers (move up the demand curve).\n(ii) Voluntary unemployment <b>falls</b>: the higher wage attracts people who were not willing to work before.\n(iii) Involuntary unemployment <b>rises</b>: more people want jobs (QS of labour) than firms offer (QD), and the gap is people who cannot find work.'},
{n:'8c)',q:'Voluntary vs involuntary unemployment.',a:'Voluntary: people who choose not to work at the current wage rate. Involuntary: people willing and able to work at the current wage but who cannot find a job.'},
{n:'8d)',q:'How (i) expansionary fiscal policy (ii) loose monetary policy (iii) flexible hours reduce unemployment.',a:'(i) More government spending (e.g. roads, seawalls) and/or tax cuts → more demand → firms hire more workers.\n(ii) Lower interest rates and more money supply → cheaper borrowing → more spending and investment → more production and jobs.\n(iii) Part-time work, job sharing and 4-day weeks spread the available work across more people, so more are employed.'},
{n:'9)',q:'(a) Allocative and regulatory roles (b) importance of price stability and a favourable BOP.',a:'(a)(i) Allocative: the government uses resources to provide public and merit goods (schools, hospitals, roads) that the market under-provides.\n(ii) Regulatory: the government makes laws and rules (consumer protection, licensing, controlling monopolies) so markets work fairly and safely.\n(b)(i) Price stability keeps goods affordable, protects the value of wages and savings, and gives certainty for business planning.\n(ii) A favourable BOP (export earnings ≥ import payments) builds foreign reserves, supports the exchange rate, and reduces borrowing and dependence on aid.'}
]},
{t:'Section E – Development (27 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'Goal of development, non-indicator, developing country, World Bank, sustainability.',a:'1. <b>D</b> 2. <b>D</b> 3. <b>B</b> 4. <b>D</b> 5. <b>C</b>'},
{n:'6a)',q:'One reason Country A is developed and one reason B is developing.',a:'A: high GDP per capita and a very high HDI, with a skilled workforce mostly in services and technology.\nB: low HDI and literacy, a high birth rate, mostly agricultural and informal jobs, and reliance on remittances and raw-material exports.'},
{n:'6b)',q:'Compare the employment structures.',a:'A: mostly in the tertiary sector (services, technology), with skilled, formal jobs.\nB: mostly in the primary sector (agriculture) and informal work, with low skills and low pay.'},
{n:'6c)',q:'Difference in trade patterns.',a:'A exports finished manufactured and high-tech goods (high value) and attracts foreign workers. B exports raw materials and agricultural products (low value, unstable prices) and relies on remittances.'},
{n:'6d)',q:'How education and healthcare affect growth in A and B.',a:'A: heavy investment produces a healthy, skilled, productive workforce, high incomes and savings, so growth is strong and sustained.\nB: low literacy and poor health mean low productivity and high death rates, little saving or investment, and slow growth (a cycle of poverty).'},
{n:'7a)',q:'Total exports and trade balance.',a:'(i) Exports = 15 + 10 + 5 = <b>$30 million</b>\n(ii) Imports = 12 + 8 + 20 = $40m → balance = 30 − 40 = <b>−$10 million</b> (trade deficit)'},
{n:'7b)',q:'Birth rate and GDP per capita.',a:'(i) Birth rate = 1,250/50,000 × 1000 = <b>25 per 1000</b>\n(ii) GDP per capita = $600,000,000/50,000 = <b>$12,000</b>'},
{n:'7c)',q:'Calculate HDI.',a:'HDI = (0.75 + 0.80 + 0.65)/3 = 2.20/3 = <b>0.733</b>\n(The UNDP geometric mean gives ∛(0.75 × 0.80 × 0.65) = 0.731. Both are about 0.73: medium–high human development.)'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC Economics .pdf', note:'Worked answers, checked against the official marking schedule. ⚠ marks places where the MOE answer or the question is wrong.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Basic economic concepts (53 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1 Most productive human resource; 2 Oligopoly; 3 Reward for financial capital; 4 Opportunity cost of sitting this exam; 5 Why scarcity exists',a:'1 <b>D</b> (teacher: a skilled worker)\n2 <b>B</b> (a few firms dominate)\n3 <b>B</b> (interest)\n4 <b>A</b> (the next best thing you would have done instead)\n5 <b>B</b> (unlimited wants, limited resources)'},
{n:'6(a).',q:'Draw a PPC for fish/potatoes and one for red hats/blue hats',a:'<b>Fish and potatoes</b>: a <b>concave (bowed-out) curve</b> joining the two axes. Label the axes Fish and Potatoes, and call the curve PPC.\n<b>Red hats and blue hats</b>: a <b>straight line</b> (constant slope), because the same resources and machines make both kinds of hat equally well.'},
{n:'6(b).',q:'Why is the fish/potato PPC concave?',a:'Resources are <b>not equally suited</b> to both goods (fishermen and boats are not good farmers). Moving more resources into fish means using ones that are worse at fishing, so each extra unit of fish costs <b>more and more potatoes</b>: <b>increasing opportunity cost</b>.'},
{n:'6(c).',q:'Distinguish (i) economies vs diseconomies of scale (ii) diminishing vs increasing returns',a:'(i) <b>Economies of scale</b>: as a firm grows larger, its <b>average cost per unit falls</b> (bulk buying, specialised machines). <b>Diseconomies of scale</b>: when a firm grows too large, its <b>average cost rises</b> (poor communication, management problems).\n(ii) <b>Increasing returns</b>: adding more of a variable factor (labour) to a fixed factor makes output rise by <b>more and more</b> (specialisation). <b>Diminishing returns</b>: after a point, each extra worker adds <b>less and less</b> output, because the fixed factor gets crowded.'},
{n:'7(a).',q:'Market structure: (i) the sole electricity provider (ii) 100 small vegetable firms',a:'(i) <b>Monopoly</b>\n(ii) <b>Perfect competition</b> (or monopolistic competition, if the vegetables are differentiated)'},
{n:'7(b).',q:'One feature each of duopoly, monopsony and monopolistic competition',a:'Duopoly: <b>only two sellers</b> dominate the market.\nMonopsony: <b>only one buyer</b> (e.g. the only employer in a town).\nMonopolistic competition: <b>many firms selling slightly different (differentiated) products</b>, e.g. by brand, with easy entry.'},
{n:'7(c).',q:'"The electricity distributor has more control over price than vegetable sellers." Describe this.',a:'The electricity distributor is the <b>only seller</b> and has no close substitutes, so it is a <b>price maker</b>: it can set its price, and people must still buy. Each vegetable seller is one of many selling the same product, so it is a <b>price taker</b>. If it charged more, buyers would go to another seller.'},
{n:'7(d).',q:'Derived demand vs final demand',a:'<b>Final demand</b>: demand for goods and services by consumers for their own use (e.g. tinned fish).\n<b>Derived demand</b>: demand for a <b>factor of production</b> that comes from the demand for what it helps to make (e.g. demand for fishermen comes from demand for fish).'},
{n:'7(e).',q:'What is exchanged in a factor market? Give an example.',a:'<b>Factors of production</b> (land, labour, capital, enterprise) are bought and sold, and paid rent, wages, interest and profit. Example: the <b>labour market</b>, where workers sell their labour for wages.'},
{n:'7(f).',q:'"Demand for fish by the tin fish market is a derived demand."',a:'The tinned-fish factory does not want fish for itself. It wants fish <b>because consumers demand tinned fish</b>. If demand for tinned fish rises, the factory\'s demand for raw fish rises too.'},
{n:'8(a).',q:'How is market supply derived?',a:'By <b>adding up the quantities supplied by all individual sellers</b> at each price (horizontal summation of the individual supply curves).'},
{n:'8(b).',q:'Banana schedule calculations',a:'Equilibrium: $6, where Qd = Qs = 25 kg.\n(i) Market revenue = P × Q = $6 × 25 = <b>$150</b>\n(ii) Excess demand at $4 = 30 − 20 = <b>10 kg</b>\n(iii) Consumer spending at $10 = $10 × 20 = <b>$200</b> (consumers only buy 20 kg)\n(iv) Excess supply at $10 = 35 − 20 = <b>15 kg</b>'},
{n:'8(c).',q:'Excess demand vs excess supply',a:'<b>Excess demand (shortage)</b>: the price is <b>below</b> equilibrium, so Qd > Qs.\n<b>Excess supply (surplus)</b>: the price is <b>above</b> equilibrium, so Qs > Qd.'},
{n:'8(d).',q:'Banana production subsidy: (i) effects (ii) new consumer spending',a:'⚠ The question says the subsidy gives "10 <i>fewer</i> units". That is a mistake: a subsidy lowers costs, so it <b>increases</b> supply by 10 kg at each price (the MOE answer uses this).\nNew supply: $2→20, $4→30, $6→35, $8→40, $10→45.\n(i) The supply curve shifts <b>right</b>. At the old price of $6 there is now a surplus (35 > 25), so the price falls. The new equilibrium is <b>$4 and 30 kg</b>: lower price, more bananas bought and sold.\n(ii) Consumer spending = $4 × 30 = <b>$120</b>'},
{n:'8(e).',q:'Tinned fish: show the effect of each situation',a:'1. Cheaper cans → production costs fall → <b>supply shifts right (S1)</b>: price falls, quantity rises.\n2. Chicken (a substitute) is unsafe → people switch to tinned fish → <b>demand shifts right (D1)</b>: price and quantity rise.\n3. A cyclone destroys the fishing fleet → less fish → <b>supply shifts left (S1)</b>: price rises, quantity falls.'},
{n:'9(a).',q:'One advantage and one disadvantage of GDP at current prices for measuring living standards',a:'Advantage: it is <b>easy to calculate</b> and shows the total value of output in today\'s money.\nDisadvantage: it <b>includes inflation</b>. GDP can rise just because prices rose, even if no more goods were produced. It also ignores population size.'},
{n:'9(b).',q:'Why is GDP at constant prices better?',a:'It values output at <b>base-year prices</b>, which <b>removes the effect of inflation</b>. A rise in real GDP therefore shows a real increase in the quantity of goods and services produced.'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Money (33 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1 Savings/wealth function; 2 Central function; 3 Why a cheque is money; 4 Near money; 5 Two-year bond',a:'1 <b>A</b> (store of value)\n2 <b>C</b> (medium of exchange)\n3 <b>C</b> (generally accepted)\n4 <b>B</b> (assets easily changed to cash)\n5 <b>C</b> (M3: longer-term)'},
{n:'6(a).',q:'Fisher\'s equation of exchange',a:'<b>MV = PQ</b> (or MV = PT)\nM = money supply, V = velocity of circulation, P = price level, Q = quantity of output.'},
{n:'6(b).',q:'What does the quantity theory imply?',a:'If V and Q stay constant, an increase in the <b>money supply causes an equal percentage rise in the price level</b>. Printing too much money causes inflation.'},
{n:'6(c).',q:'Q = 30 000, P = $15, M = $100 000. Find (i) nominal GDP (ii) V',a:'(i) Nominal GDP = P × Q = $15 × 30 000 = <b>$450 000</b>\n(ii) V = PQ/M = 450 000/100 000 = <b>4.5 times</b>'},
{n:'6(d).',q:'Money supply and inflation',a:'A <b>direct (positive) relationship</b>: with V and Q fixed, MV = PQ means a bigger M must give a bigger P. More money chasing the same amount of goods pushes prices up.'},
{n:'7(a).',q:'Cause, effect or measure of inflation?',a:'(i) Rising wage or raw-material costs: <b>C</b> (cost-push)\n(ii) Increase in demand: <b>C</b> (demand-pull)\n(iii) Redistributes income: <b>E</b>\n(iv) Harms people on fixed incomes: <b>E</b>\n(v) Government cuts its spending: <b>M</b>\n(vi) Central bank reduces cash settlement: <b>M</b>'},
{n:'7(b).',q:'Demand-pull or cost-push?',a:'(i) Petrol price doubles: <b>cost-push</b>\n(ii) Lower direct tax: <b>demand-pull</b> (people have more to spend)\n(iii) Higher wage rates: <b>cost-push</b>\n(iv) Budget deficit: <b>demand-pull</b>\n(v) Dearer raw materials: <b>cost-push</b>'},
{n:'7(c).',q:'7.3% inflation: impact on low-income households, savers and borrowers',a:'<b>Low-income households</b>: worse off. Their incomes don\'t rise as fast as prices, so they can buy less, and they spend most of their income on necessities.\n<b>Savers</b>: worse off. The <b>real value of their savings falls</b> (unless the interest rate is above 7.3%).\n<b>Borrowers</b>: better off. They repay loans with money that is <b>worth less</b> than when they borrowed it.'},
{n:'7(d)(i).',q:'One other function of the Reserve Bank',a:'e.g. <b>Issues the notes and coins</b> (currency); <b>banker to the government</b> (keeps its accounts, manages its debt); <b>banker to the commercial banks</b> (lender of last resort); manages foreign reserves.'},
{n:'7(d)(ii).',q:'How does moral suasion help price stability?',a:'Moral suasion means the Reserve Bank <b>persuades or advises</b> commercial banks, without passing a law, e.g. to lend less or be careful with credit. Less lending means less money and spending in the economy, so <b>demand-pull inflation is reduced</b>.'}
]},
{t:'Section C – International trade and payments (40 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1 Fall in terms of trade; 2 $AU1 in $NZ; 3 Floating rate; 4 Effects of a tariff; 5 NOT an invisible',a:'1 <b>B</b> (export prices fell more than import prices)\n2 <b>C</b>: 1/0.90 = $NZ1.11\n3 <b>C</b> (supply and demand)\n4 <b>D</b> (or B). ⚠ The MOE answer is A, but a tariff <b>raises</b> consumer prices. A tariff causes a loss of consumer surplus (B) and benefits domestic producers (D).\n5 <b>C</b> (borrowing is in the capital account)'},
{n:'6(a).',q:'Absolute advantage in (i) beef (ii) cassava',a:'(i) Beef: <b>Sweet Islands</b> (1500 > 1000)\n(ii) Cassava: <b>Sweet Islands</b> (2100 > 2000)'},
{n:'6(b).',q:'Banana Islands: opportunity cost of (i) 1 t cassava (ii) 1 t beef',a:'(i) 1 t cassava = 1000/2000 = <b>0.5 t beef</b>\n(ii) 1 t beef = 2000/1000 = <b>2 t cassava</b>'},
{n:'6(c).',q:'Sweet Islands: opportunity cost',a:'(i) 1 t cassava = 1500/2100 = <b>0.71 t beef</b>\n(ii) 1 t beef = 2100/1500 = <b>1.4 t cassava</b>'},
{n:'6(d).',q:'Gains from trade for Sweet Islands at 1 beef : 3 cassava',a:'Sweet Islands has the lower opportunity cost for beef (1.4 < 2 cassava), so it has the <b>comparative advantage in beef</b>. Banana Islands has it in cassava (0.5 < 0.71 beef).\nSweet Islands specialises: 1500 t beef → trades it at 1 : 3 → 1500 × 3 = 4500 t cassava\nWithout trade it could make only 2100 t cassava\nGain = 4500 − 2100 = <b>2400 t cassava</b>\n(Note: a realistic rate must lie between 1.4 and 2 cassava per beef. At 1 : 3, Banana Islands would lose, but use the rate given.)'},
{n:'7(a).',q:'"The Iceland dollar has appreciated against the Waterland dollar"',a:'One IC dollar now <b>buys more Waterland dollars</b>: its value rose from WA$0.70 to <b>WA$0.95</b>.'},
{n:'7(b).',q:'One reason for the appreciation',a:'<b>Demand for IC$ increased</b> (D shifted to D\'), e.g. more foreigners buying Iceland\'s exports, more tourists visiting Iceland, higher interest rates attracting foreign savings, or more foreign investment.'},
{n:'7(c).',q:'Impact on Iceland\'s balance of trade',a:'Iceland\'s <b>exports become dearer</b> to foreigners, so it sells fewer, while <b>imports become cheaper</b>, so it buys more. The balance of trade <b>worsens</b> (moves towards a deficit).'},
{n:'7(d).',q:'IC importers buying WA$200 of machines: (i) before (ii) after',a:'IC$1 = WA$0.70 before and WA$0.95 after.\n(i) Before: 200 ÷ 0.70 = <b>IC$285.71</b>\n(ii) After: 200 ÷ 0.95 = <b>IC$210.53</b>\nSo imports are now <b>cheaper</b> for Iceland.\n⚠ The MOE schedule uses 0.75 and multiplies (giving IC$150 then IC$190), which is wrong. The graph shows 0.70, and after an appreciation imports must get <i>cheaper</i>, not dearer.'},
{n:'8(a).',q:'Base year',a:'<b>Year 2</b> (both indices = 100).'},
{n:'8(b).',q:'Terms of trade each year',a:'ToT = export price index ÷ import price index × 100\nYear 1: 90/95 × 100 = <b>95</b>\nYear 2: 100/100 × 100 = <b>100</b>\nYear 3: 106/102 × 100 = <b>104</b>'},
{n:'8(c).',q:'Benefit of an improvement in the terms of trade',a:'Each unit of exports now <b>buys more imports</b>. The country can import more machines, fuel and food for the same amount of exports. This raises living standards and can improve the balance of payments.'},
{n:'8(d).',q:'One cause of a favourable movement',a:'<b>Export prices rise</b> (e.g. the world price of copra or fish goes up), or <b>import prices fall</b> (e.g. cheaper fuel).'},
{n:'9(a).',q:'Two economic effects of the EU grant',a:'1. Builds <b>human capital</b>: better schools and technical training make a more skilled and productive workforce.\n2. Health and sanitation projects make a <b>healthier workforce</b>, with fewer sick days and lower health costs for the government. They also create jobs during construction.'},
{n:'9(b).',q:'Foreign aid vs foreign investment',a:'<b>Foreign aid</b>: grants, soft loans or technical help given by other governments or agencies, usually <b>not for profit</b>.\n<b>Foreign investment</b>: foreign firms or people putting money into businesses or assets in the country <b>to earn a profit</b> (e.g. a hotel or fishing company).'}
]},
{t:'Section D – Economic functions of government (47 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1 Fair income role; 2 Countering recession; 3 NOT a type of intervention; 4 Example of intervention; 5 Intervention for fair income',a:'1 <b>B</b> (distributive)\n2 <b>C</b> (stabilisation)\n3 <b>D</b> (stabilisation is a role, not a type of intervention)\n4 <b>A</b> (an age limit for alcohol)\n5 <b>C</b> (income support)'},
{n:'6(a).',q:'Taxation method in Country Y and Country Z',a:'Country Y: the rate <b>rises</b> as income rises → <b>progressive</b>\nCountry Z (printed as "X"): the rate <b>falls</b> as income rises → <b>regressive</b>'},
{n:'6(b).',q:'Two features of a good tax system',a:'Any two: <b>equity (fairness)</b>, <b>certainty</b> (people know what to pay), <b>convenience</b> (easy to pay), <b>economy</b> (cheap to collect), simplicity.'},
{n:'6(c).',q:'Tax in Country Y',a:'(i) Amon: $3500 × 0.30 = <b>$1050</b>\n(ii) Meteka: $850 × 0.20 = <b>$170</b>\n(iii) Terira: $2020 × 0.26 = <b>$525.20</b>'},
{n:'7(a).',q:'Expansionary/contractionary and monetary/fiscal',a:'(i) Increase income tax: <b>C, F</b>\n(ii) Increase health spending: <b>E, F</b>\n(iii) Reduce reserve asset ratio: <b>E, M</b>\n(iv) Sell government securities: <b>C, M</b>'},
{n:'7(b).',q:'Distinguish the fiscal and monetary versions',a:'(i) <b>Expansionary fiscal</b>: the government <b>cuts taxes or raises its spending</b> to boost demand. <b>Expansionary monetary</b>: the central bank <b>increases the money supply or lowers interest rates</b>.\n(ii) <b>Contractionary fiscal</b>: <b>higher taxes or less government spending</b>. <b>Contractionary monetary</b>: <b>less money supply or higher interest rates</b>, to slow spending and inflation.'},
{n:'7(c).',q:'How each measure affects price stability',a:'(i) Higher income tax → less disposable income → less spending → <b>lower inflation</b>.\n(ii) More health spending → more total demand → can <b>raise inflation</b>.\n(iii) Lower reserve ratio → banks can lend more → more money supply → can <b>raise inflation</b>.\n(iv) Selling securities → takes money out of the economy → less spending → <b>lower inflation</b>.'},
{n:'8(a).',q:'Voluntary vs involuntary unemployment',a:'<b>Voluntary</b>: people choose not to work at the current wage (e.g. waiting for a better job, studying).\n<b>Involuntary</b>: people are <b>willing and able to work</b> at the current wage but <b>cannot find a job</b>.'},
{n:'8(b).',q:'How each measure reduces unemployment',a:'(i) <b>Lower the retirement age</b>: older workers leave, which opens jobs for young job seekers.\n(ii) <b>More government spending</b> (e.g. on roads and schools) creates jobs directly and raises demand, so firms hire more.\n(iii) <b>Raise the school-leaving age</b>: young people stay in school longer, so fewer enter the labour force, and they become more skilled and employable.'},
{n:'8(c).',q:'Expansionary fiscal policy and unemployment',a:'Lower taxes or more government spending → higher aggregate demand → firms sell more and <b>hire more workers</b> → <b>unemployment falls</b>.'},
{n:'8(d).',q:'Subsidies to businesses',a:'Subsidies <b>lower production costs</b>, so firms can produce more, expand and <b>employ more workers</b>. More output means higher GDP: <b>economic growth</b>.'}
]},
{t:'Section E – Economic development (27 marks)', qs:[
{n:'MCQ 1–5',q:'1 Industry common in LDCs; 2 Best indicator of living standards; 3 Expanding productive capacity; 4 NOT a benefit of growth; 5 NOT a feature of an LDC',a:'1 <b>A</b> (primary)\n2 <b>D</b> (real GDP per capita). ⚠ The MOE schedule says B, but real GDP per capita is the best measure of the standard of living because it accounts for both inflation and population.\n3 <b>D</b> (more resources)\n4 <b>A</b>\n5 <b>D</b> (ample skilled labour)'},
{n:'6(a).',q:'Death rate and migration in an LDC',a:'(i) <b>Death rate</b>: deaths per 1000 people per year. It is high in LDCs (poor health care, poor sanitation, high infant mortality) but often falling.\n(ii) <b>Migration</b>: people moving out of or into a country (or from outer islands to towns). LDCs often lose skilled workers to richer countries (a <b>brain drain</b>), and many move from rural areas to towns (urbanisation, e.g. to South Tarawa).'},
{n:'6(b).',q:'Income per capita; poverty',a:'(i) <b>Income per capita</b>: the national income divided by the population, i.e. the average income per person.\n(ii) <b>Poverty</b>: not having enough income or resources for <b>basic needs</b> (food, shelter, clean water, health, education).'},
{n:'6(c).',q:'Which island is more likely to grow?',a:'<b>Left Island</b>. It produces more <b>capital goods</b> (8 million): machines, buildings and tools that increase future productive capacity and shift its PPC outwards. Right Island produces mostly consumption goods, which are used up now.'},
{n:'6(d).',q:'Using education to judge which island is more developed',a:'Compare the <b>literacy rate</b> and <b>school enrolment</b> (especially secondary and tertiary), the years of schooling and the number of trained teachers. The island with higher literacy and more educated people has more human capital, so it is <b>more developed</b>.'},
{n:'7(a).',q:'Encourage or discourage growth?',a:'(i) Professionals emigrate (brain drain): <b>discourage</b>\n(ii) Young people start commercial farming: <b>encourage</b>\n(iii) Better education facilities: <b>encourage</b>\n(iv) Depressed world export prices: <b>discourage</b>'},
{n:'7(b).',q:'Developing or developed?',a:'(i) Low share of GDP saved for investment: <b>developing</b>\n(ii) Exports mainly manufactured goods: <b>developed</b>\n(iii) Little or no foreign aid received: <b>developed</b>\n(iv) Mainly uneducated labour force: <b>developing</b>'},
{n:'8(a).',q:'Highland: (i) GDP per capita (ii) foreign aid : GDP',a:'(i) $500m ÷ 1.5m people = <b>$333.33 per person</b>\n(ii) $20m : $500m = <b>1 : 25</b> (= 4% of GDP)\n⚠ The MOE schedule writes "$2m : $55m", which is wrong. Simplify 20 : 500 by dividing both by 20.'},
{n:'8(b).',q:'Why is literacy so high (98%)?',a:'Highland is a developed country with <b>high income</b>, so the government can afford <b>free, compulsory education</b>, good schools and well-trained teachers. Its large tertiary (service) sector also needs educated workers.'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/ECONOMICS EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the places where the scheme is wrong. The DVD schedule figures are read from the scheme because the table is cut off in the printed paper.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Basic concepts', qs:[
{n:'MC 1–5',q:'PPC of taro and sugar',a:'1 <b>D</b> maximum combinations produced · 2 <b>D</b> points on the curve (C and D) · 3 <b>B</b> beyond the curve (unattainable) · 4 <b>A</b> less sugar · 5 <b>A</b> inside the curve'},
{n:'6.',q:'Factor rewards; factors in the boat example',a:'(a) Timber and thatch → <b>rent</b>; manager → <b>profit</b>; workers → <b>wages</b>; tools → <b>interest</b>.\n(b) Business person → <b>enterprise</b>; boat → <b>capital</b>; fisherman → <b>labour</b>; fish in the sea (natural resource) → <b>land</b>. (The scheme also accepts "local market" for land.)'},
{n:'7.',q:'Circular flow model',a:'(a) It shows how money (and goods, services and resources) flow between the sectors of the economy.\n(b) It trades with other countries: the <b>overseas sector</b> buys exports (X, an injection) and sells imports (M, a leakage).\n(c) Households <b>save</b> part of their income in banks (a leakage, S); producers <b>borrow</b> these savings to buy capital goods – investment (an injection, I).\n(d) Government collects taxes (T) from households and firms and spends (G) on public goods, services and transfer payments.\n(e) (ii) <b>G</b> · (iii) <b>M</b> · (iv) <b>I</b> · (v) <b>S</b>'},
{n:'8.',q:'DVD market: equilibrium, spending, price floor, sales tax',a:'(a) Equilibrium is where <b>quantity demanded = quantity supplied</b> ($1.20, 1940 DVDs).\n(b) Spending = P × Q = $1.20 × 1940 = <b>$2328</b>\n(c) At $1.40, Qd = 1910 and Qs = 1990 → a <b>surplus of 80 DVDs</b> (excess supply).\n(d) Supply shifts left (up by $0.20): equilibrium price <b>rises</b> and quantity <b>falls</b> (to 1925).\n(e)(i) Before: $2328. After: suppliers keep $1.05 per DVD → 1.05 × 1925 = $2021.25. Change = <b>−$306.75</b> (a fall).\n(ii) Tax revenue = $0.20 × 1925 = <b>$385</b>\n(f) Lower income tax → higher disposable income → demand for DVDs <b>increases</b> (curve shifts right).\n(g) Change in demand = whole curve shifts because of a non-price factor (income, tastes). Change in quantity demanded = movement along the curve because the good\'s own price changes.'},
{n:'9.',q:'GDP by income and expenditure; real GDP',a:'(a) GDP = market value of all final goods and services produced in a country in one year.\n(b)(i) Income: W + P + D + NIT = 33 000 + 22 000 + 6 000 + 10 000 = <b>$71 000 m</b>\n(ii) Expenditure: C + G + I + ΔStock + (X − M) = 44 000 + 12 000 + 14 000 + 2 000 + (19 000 − 20 000) = <b>$71 000 m</b>\n(c)(i) Real GDP = nominal × 1000/CPI. 2000: 109 696 × 1000/870 = <b>$126 087.36 m</b>; 2006: 155 554 × 1000/1005 = <b>$154 780.10 m</b>\n(ii) Real GDP grew by 22.8% – real output increased (growth after removing inflation).\n⚠ The MOE scheme says the growth "is due to inflation"; that is wrong, because real GDP has already had inflation removed.\n(d) Nominal GDP is valued at <b>current prices</b>; real GDP is valued at <b>constant (base-year) prices</b>, so it is adjusted for inflation.'},
{n:'10.',q:'AS–AD shifts',a:'(i) Cheaper imported raw materials → <b>AS shifts right</b> → price level falls, output rises.\n(ii) Higher net exports → <b>AD shifts right</b> → price level and output rise.\n(iii) Fall in business confidence → less investment → <b>AD shifts left</b> → price level and output fall.\nLabel the new curves AS₁/AD₁, and mark the new P₁ and Y₁.'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Money', qs:[
{n:'MC 1–5',q:'Quantity theory; Reserve Bank',a:'1 <b>D</b> MV = PQ · 2 <b>A</b> velocity · 3 <b>D</b> · 4 <b>B</b> · 5 <b>C</b> personal loans (note: fiscal policy, D, is also the government\'s job, not the Reserve Bank\'s)'},
{n:'6.',q:'Functions/characteristics; store vs standard of value; John',a:'(a) (i) characteristic (scarcity) · (ii) function (standard of value) · (iii) function (deferred payment) · (iv) characteristic (recognisable)\n(b) Store of value: money can be saved and used later. Standard of value: money measures and compares the value of goods and services.\n(c) Medium of exchange – his $10 spent on groceries. Store of value – saving $20 for school expenses. Standard of value – his work is valued at $30 a week ($1000 later). Deferred payment – paying the hire-purchase account.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Government borrowing and money supply; M1, near money, M3; liquidity',a:'(a)(i) From the public: people pay with existing money, which the government then spends back into the economy – <b>no overall change</b> in money supply.\n(ii) From the central bank: new money is created and spent → money supply <b>increases</b>.\n(b) Narrow money = 605 + 265 = <b>$870</b>; near money (M2) = 870 + 520 = <b>$1390</b>; broad money (M3) = 1390 + 3500 = <b>$4890</b>\n(c) Notes and coins → transaction accounts → savings accounts → other deposits.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Inflation from base year; trend; monetary tools',a:'(a)(i) Year 2: (120 − 100)/100 × 100 = <b>20%</b>\n(ii) Year 4: (165 − 100)/100 × 100 = <b>65%</b> from the base year\n⚠ The MOE scheme works out (165 − 132)/100 = 33%, which is wrong. That figure is neither the change from the base year (65%) nor year 3 to year 4 (25%).\n(b) Prices rose every year; the yearly rate slowed from 20% (Y1→Y2) to 10% (Y2→Y3) then speeded up to 25% (Y3→Y4).\n(c)(i) <b>Raise the OCR</b> → interest rates rise → less borrowing, more saving → spending and AD fall → price level falls.\n(ii) <b>Sell government bonds</b> (OMO) → cash leaves the banks → less money to lend → money supply and AD fall.\n(iii) <b>Raise the reserve asset ratio</b> → banks must hold more reserves → less lending → money supply and AD fall.'}
]},
{t:'Section C – International trade', qs:[
{n:'MC 1–5',q:'Bilateral agreement; marketing boards; floating rate; IMF',a:'1 <b>D</b> CER (Australia–NZ, two countries) – PICTA is regional\n⚠ The MOE key gives C, which is wrong.\n2 <b>C</b> · 3 <b>D</b> · 4 <b>A</b> · 5 <b>C</b>'},
{n:'6.',q:'Absolute and comparative advantage',a:'(a) B: vegetables 8, meat 4. C: vegetables 2, meat 6.\n(b) <b>Country B</b>\n(c) Opportunity cost of 1 vegetable: B = 4/8 = 0.5 meat; C = 6/2 = 3 meat → <b>B</b> (lower cost).\n(d) Opportunity cost of 1 meat: B = 8/4 = 2 vegetables; C = 2/6 = 0.33 vegetables → <b>C</b>.\n(e) Absolute advantage: produces more with the same resources. Comparative advantage: produces at a lower opportunity cost.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Exchange rate graph; conversions; shifts',a:'(a) The NZ$ <b>depreciated</b> (ER* → ER¹, supply of NZ$ increased).\n(b) NZ exports become cheaper overseas → exports <b>increase</b>.\n(c) More NZ$ supplied, e.g. NZ people importing more, travelling overseas or investing abroad.\n(d)(i) 120 ÷ 0.84 = <b>NZ$142.86</b> (ii) 70 ÷ 0.57 = <b>NZ$122.81</b>\n(e) Situation 1: higher Australian interest rates → NZ investors buy A$ → <b>supply of NZ$ shifts right</b> → NZ$ falls. Situation 2: NZ tourists in Australia sell NZ$ for A$ → <b>supply of NZ$ shifts right</b> → NZ$ falls.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Balance of payments',a:'(a)(i) Current account = trade in goods + services + investment income + transfers. (ii) Capital account = official + non-official capital flows (loans, investment).\n(b)(i) A good is a physical item sent to another country; a service is non-physical work sold to foreigners (e.g. tourism, shipping).\n(ii) Balance of trade = 164 − 184 = <b>−$20 m</b>; transfers = 15 − 22 = <b>−$7 m</b>\nCurrent account = −20 + (60 − 48) + (105 − 95) − 7 = −20 + 12 + 10 − 7 = <b>−$5 m</b>\nCapital account = (14 − 31) + (25 − 55) = −17 − 30 = <b>−$47 m</b>\n(c) More money flows out than in; spending goes to foreign producers, so local firms sell less and may lay off workers (employment falls), and economic growth slows. The deficit must be financed by borrowing or reserves.'}
]},
{t:'Section D – Government', qs:[
{n:'MC 1–5',q:'Non-economic function; tax rise; unemployment role; COVID job loss; not a benefit',a:'1 <b>A</b> · 2 <b>B</b> fiscal · 3 <b>D</b> stabilising · 4 <b>A</b> cyclical · 5 <b>D</b> higher price level'},
{n:'6.',q:'Interventions; taxes vs subsidies; measures that hurt each aim',a:'(a) (i) subsidies · (ii) direct provision · (iii) regulations · (iv) taxes\n(b) Taxes are compulsory payments to government (raise prices / reduce consumption); subsidies are government payments to producers that lower costs and prices.\n(c)(i) Economic growth – decrease government expenditure (<b>fiscal</b>)\nPrice stability – increase pensions (more spending, <b>fiscal</b>)\nFair income distribution – progressive → proportional tax (<b>fiscal</b>)\nFavourable balance of payments – appreciation of the exchange rate (<b>monetary</b>)\nFull employment – raise interest rates (<b>monetary</b>)\n(Growth and full employment can be swapped.) ⚠ The MOE scheme calls the exchange-rate appreciation fiscal; it is a Reserve Bank (monetary) measure, so that is wrong.\n(ii) Fiscal policy = government taxing and spending (the budget); monetary policy = Reserve Bank control of money supply and interest rates.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Evergreen Land budget',a:'(a)(i) Budget balance = revenue − expenditure = 9.5 − 10.0 = <b>−$0.5 m (deficit)</b>\n(ii) Tax = 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 2.0 + 0.5 = 5.0 → 5.0/9.5 × 100 = <b>52.6%</b>\n(b) Direct: income tax (or company tax). Indirect: sales tax (or duty).\n(c) Direct tax is on income, paid straight to the government by the person earning it; indirect tax is on spending, collected through sellers.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Inequality; transfer payments; Lorenz curve',a:'(a) Income is shared unequally – a few earn a lot while many earn little.\n(b) Transfer payments are government payments to people (pensions, unemployment and family benefits) paid for by taxes, especially on higher incomes, which moves income from rich to poor.\n⚠ The MOE scheme describes subsidies to producers instead; that is wrong.\n(c)(i) Curve A (the diagonal) is perfect equality; the actual curve lies below it, so income is unequally distributed – the further it bows away, the greater the inequality.\n(ii) Curve C drawn <b>between</b> the actual curve and line A (closer to equality).'},
{n:'9.',q:'Participation and unemployment rates; policy; types',a:'(a) Labour force = 1 500 000 + 400 000 + 100 000 = 2 000 000; working-age = 3 000 000\n(i) Participation = 2 000 000/3 000 000 × 100 = <b>67%</b> (ii) Unemployment = 100 000/2 000 000 × 100 = <b>5%</b>\n(b) Expansionary fiscal policy (more spending, lower taxes) raises AD; firms produce more and hire more workers → unemployment falls.\n(c) (i) frictional (seasonal job) · (ii) cyclical · (iii) structural\n(d) Lowering the retirement age makes older workers leave, opening jobs for unemployed people and school leavers.'}
]},
{t:'Section E – Development', qs:[
{n:'MC 1–5',q:'Birth rate; real GDP per capita; developing economy; growth; main problem',a:'1 <b>D</b> · 2 <b>A</b> – real GDP per capita is a measure of living standards, so low values show poverty (the MOE key gives D) · 3 <b>A</b> · 4 <b>A</b> · 5 <b>A</b>'},
{n:'6a–d.',q:'Developed vs developing; savings/GDP; birth rate; Countries Y and Z',a:'(a) Developed: high income per head, industrial and service economy, high living standards. Developing: low income per head, mostly primary production, lower living standards.\n(b) Work out savings ÷ GDP: developed countries save a higher share of GDP; developing countries save a low share.\n(c) Birth rates are low (family planning, education, cost of children).\n(d)(i) Under-5 mortality = deaths of children under 5 per 1000 live births in a year. Remittances = money sent home by people working overseas.\n(ii) <b>Country Y</b> (iii) lower child mortality (better health care) and less dependence on remittances.'},
{n:'6e.',q:'Country A calculations',a:'Total GDP = 40 + 5 + 5 = <b>$50 m</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme adds foreign aid ($25 m) instead of tertiary production, giving $70 m, which is wrong. Aid is not production.\nLabour force % = 500 000/1 000 000 × 100 = <b>50%</b>\nForeign aid : GDP = 25 : 50 = <b>1 : 2</b> (50%)\nPrimary share = 40/50 × 100 = <b>80%</b>'},
{n:'7.',q:'Poverty traps; infrastructure; education and health',a:'(a) A cycle where low income → low savings and investment → low productivity → low income again, so a country cannot escape poverty on its own.\n(b)(i) Roads, ports, power and internet cut transport costs, link producers to markets and attract investment, raising output and incomes.\n(ii) Educated, healthy workers are more skilled and productive, earn more and live longer, which raises GDP and living standards.'}
]}
]}
]});
